00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:47.228
actually doing some EDA, maybe
identifying the points of data

00:06:47.228 --> 00:06:49.520
that you're actually going
to use to build models, what

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:50.770
models are you going to build.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:55.550
And then you go into the
heavy compute costly piece

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:59.180
of the workflow, which is
where you train your models.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:01.790
And you keep iterating
that until you get the best

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:03.770
model-- your optimal model.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:06.740
That's where you'll
see sleepless nights

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.740
and several days spent
by a coffee field

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:15.530
data scientists just trying
to get the best model out.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:17.728
Once that model is
out, maybe you're

00:07:17.728 --> 00:07:20.270
going to attach, at the end of
it, the [INAUDIBLE] processing

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:22.700
phase, another ML workload.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.770
You may classify something.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:26.450
You may forecast something.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:28.220
Or you're just going
to use heuristics

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:30.380
to detect an anomaly,
for instance,

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:34.060
in a predictive
maintenance use case.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:35.800
And once you tune
that detectability,

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.630
once you tune that
classification,

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:41.480
you can deploy your metric.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:45.020
Now if you're very lucky,
maybe you'll get user feedback.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:48.860
And that's where you start
iterating over your cycles

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:49.790
and your loops.

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:50.990
So you get user feedback.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:52.490
You retune your detectability.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.060
And you're good to go.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.520
If you're not that
lucky, maybe you

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:58.790
need to retrain, a little
bit, pieces of your models.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:02.180
So you go one step back,
maybe use transfer learning,

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:05.100
and update parts of your
deep learning model.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.430
And if you're not
lucky at all, you

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:11.960
go all the way back and have to,
basically, build a new model.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:13.790
Try going and telling
the data scientists

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:18.110
that spent weeks on end that
that model was not great.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:20.880
And we're going to have
to start all over again.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.660
It's kind of complicated,
but sometimes happens.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:28.470
So that's the reason
why, at Baker, we

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:32.130
started looking into ways
to automate this workflow

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:37.650
and give the data science a
more repeatable and scalable way

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.330
to build those models.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.429
So when we tackle a
platform like the one

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:45.150
I showed before, we can build
several models in parallel,

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.660
instead of every time building
one model, and then two weeks

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.490
later, starting the next one.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.750
That doesn't scale.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:54.600
So let me talk about
that first loop

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.610
that we saw about
training models.

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.250
So we've, at Baker
Hughes, came up

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:03.840
with this probabilistic
evolutionary framework

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.830
of optimizing architectures
of deep learning models.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:12.270
So when we go about building
TensorFlow based models

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.363
to detect anomaly,
for instance, we

00:09:14.363 --> 00:09:15.780
don't want to just
go and randomly

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.900
search all the space available,
and just go and try to tune

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.430
the hyperparameters.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:24.390
We want to make sure we
found our global best

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:27.540
model over parameter
combinations,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.760
over architecture combinations.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.010
And for that, what we do is
once we've built the model

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:35.280
and we explore,
intelligently, a space,

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.130
we build a meta-model of
the model's performance.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.960
And then we apply
evolutionary methods

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.880
to that meta-model, which
is cheaper to compute.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.230
So you start with red
dots, which are actually

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:50.190
heavy GPU, costly compute.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:52.650
Then you create a meta-model.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:54.580
And you apply the
evolutionary methods.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:57.240
And then you just go and
burst these gray dots

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:58.833
and try to find the optimum.

00:09:58.833 --> 00:10:00.750
If you keep doing that,
if you keep iterating,

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:04.980
if you keep the cycle, you
go from black to blue to red

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.650
to green, where
you get the minimum

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:12.360
of both of your objectives
or more than two objectives.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:16.050
This gives us a 10x improvement
over just a random trial

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.270
and error research.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:21.570
And we end up, actually,
exploring a quarter

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.460
of a million architectures.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:32.400
And this is a pretty big,
beefy, big model space.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.450
Now if you think that that is
done by custom scripts, that's

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:39.980
is also not scalable.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.770
So for that, we use a
very nice tool from Google

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:50.540
called Kubeflow, which gives me
a way to Pythonically honestly

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:52.880
describe that orchestration.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.990
So I can use Python objects
to define the operations that

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:59.247
are going to give me the
initial samples, then

00:10:59.247 --> 00:11:00.830
the operation that
is going to give me

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:03.500
the next best models
through a genetic algorithm,

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:06.440
for instance, and the next
few points I'm sampling.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:07.377
And I keep doing that.

00:11:07.377 --> 00:11:09.210
And that's what you're
seeing on the screen.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:12.110
So the first line is actually
120 deep learning models

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:16.153
being built concurrently
on a Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:16.153 --> 00:11:18.320
And here we have to be
intelligent about using GPUs.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.030
Because a single GPU
is very expensive.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:22.490
Imagine having to have 120.

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:27.380
So we can, sometimes,
have split sessions

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:30.720
and run two or three
models per GPU.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.870
And that's what we do here.

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:34.390
We go back in.

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:36.200
We optimize, find
the better points,

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.980
and keep doing that until
we find the best model.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:44.130
But still, GPU are
very expensive.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:49.070
So for that, we try to leverage
autoscaling applications

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:53.330
so that every time we need
an extra GPU, every time we

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:58.250
hit 80%, 90% of utilization
of our GPU in our clusters,

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:00.080
we give it another GPU.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.990
And we keep scaling up
until the point where

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:05.870
we finish up the experiments,
we finish up the loops.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.420
And here you see on the
bottom right hand corner

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.490
that we start with a
baseline that's probably

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:12.470
supporting other GPU workloads.

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:16.250
And as these optimization
starts, we climb.

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:18.080
And then we get them
down where we are just

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:19.910
running the genetic algorithm.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:24.330
And we keep doing that
until we shut them all down.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:27.180
This gets us to a
level of automation

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.490
of building deep
learning based models

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:33.320
that can help our customers,
now, in a reasonable time

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:36.810
frame, achieve those AI
based predictive maintenance

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.860
outcomes that they need.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:43.380
Now this is not what
scale is all about.

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:47.130
And I'll leave it up
to Alex, my colleague,

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:50.873
to dig a little bit deeper
into what scale means.

00:12:50.873 --> 00:12:52.290
ALEX IANKOULSKI:
Thank you, Fabio.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:53.200
Hi, everyone.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.980
I'm really excited to
have this opportunity

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:58.850
to talk to you
about Scale today.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:08.150
In my talk, I will
use some analogies.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:16.530
And I invite you to keep
an open mind and have fun.

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:20.650
So our challenge with
scale, to make everything

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:25.330
that Fabio just talked about
operate at really large scale.

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:30.380
Put in one word, was just
to be SMART about it.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:34.000
So we needed to support
that typical workflow

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:41.590
that Fabio showed that
allows us to continue and not

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:43.660
disrupt the productivity
and the process

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:46.510
that data scientists
are already used to.

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:50.170
We needed to maintain
freedom of choice.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:53.680
Because there is a huge
diversity of technologies

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:57.400
that have accumulated in
the oil and gas industry

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.260
over the past 30 years.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:02.530
And we have to be able
to support all of them.

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:05.320
We need to keep things
simple to ensure

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.780
adoption of the platforms
we are building.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:11.140
We have to be able to
readily package and deploy

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:14.320
to multiple targets,
including on prem,

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:17.050
data centers, and cloud.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:20.380
And we have to run
all of this at scale,

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:22.870
be able to extract
insights, and prove

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:25.720
the value of these platforms.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:30.070
So we made some choices
while building our platforms.

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:33.880
And the three major
ones are, first,

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:36.250
run everything in containers.

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:40.030
We tell our data
scientists, you can

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:43.210
do anything that you need to
do-- use the tools that you're

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:49.360
used to, the technologies that
help you get to your result.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:53.290
But to productize the
fruits of your work,

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.945
just put everything
in a container.

00:14:55.945 --> 00:14:57.820
And that is why we built
the Depend on Docker

00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:01.960
project, which makes it easy for
anyone, even people who are not

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:06.510
familiar with Docker, to put
their software in a container,

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:09.090
be it Linux or Windows.

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:13.530
The second choice we made
is, keep compute and storage

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.570
completely separate.

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:19.590
That gives us very
nice flexibility,

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:23.550
ease of deployment, and
treating the compute

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:26.310
as something that's ephemeral.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:30.000
Even if it goes completely away,
we can easily stand it back up

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.160
and bring the systems online.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:34.150
It also allows us to scale.

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:36.780
And the third choice
is to orchestrate

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:39.390
all of these containers
using Kubernetes.

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:44.600
Today we're going to see a demo.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:46.440
And for the purposes
of this demo,

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.050
we'll look at this use case.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:56.240
We'll start with a data
file that has 20 variables.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:00.475
And that is really a
collection of 20 time series.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.980
So the answer that
we'll be looking

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:10.000
for is, what is the
very best model that you

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:13.960
can build using this data set?

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:20.620
Each one of these variables
can be the output variable.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:24.100
And each one of the
rest of the variables

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:28.880
can be used to predict
that output variable.

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:35.180
So we'll go through
this workflow

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:43.610
to generate a large number
of model building jobs

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:49.250
to produce possible
models that can

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:51.500
be built with that data set.

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:57.070
And just for one file that
has 20 variables, when

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.750
all the combinations
are put together--

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:02.980
and we're also using
different modeling techniques.

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:06.670
So here, we're going to use six
different modeling techniques.

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:10.540
So for 20 variables with
6 modeling techniques,

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:14.230
you get 120 models
that you can build.

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:22.300
To show scale, we're also
going to simulate 10 users

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:26.980
using 10 of these data files,
simultaneously, producing

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:29.710
a total of 1,200 models.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:33.310
And that is a very, very
small use case for us.

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:39.870
To show you a little bit
about the architecture

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:43.100
we've put together to help
us do that, we basically

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:44.330
have three layers--

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:47.960
a storage layer, a layer
with core services,

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:52.070
and the run times.

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:55.420
So for the core services,
there is one thing

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:56.680
that I want to point out.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.420
One of the lessons learned
when we were working on scaling

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:11.750
this up is that it is
necessary to break down

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:15.560
the services from
generalized services

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:22.130
to specialized services so each
individual microservice can

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:24.120
scale better.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:28.850
So in the beginning, we had
the generic task service.

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:35.180
And that got split
into several components

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:38.270
of that task service,
which is, the first one,

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:42.180
responsible for watching
the Kubernetes' API.

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:46.370
The next one, responsible
for creating model jobs.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:51.140
The next one, responsible for
collecting the results from

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.000
jobs that have completed.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:57.165
The last one is responsible for
cleaning up after everybody.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:06.120
So I can take the whole
time of this talk just going

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.650
over the points in this slide.

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:12.760
But I like doing things a lot
more than talking about them.

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:15.450
So we're just going to
hit the high points.

00:19:15.450 --> 00:19:19.650
Lessons we've learned
from this exercise.

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:23.580
Large scale doesn't
come out of the box.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:27.190
You will be required
to work for it.

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:29.520
And it's not a manual thing.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:32.640
If you try to repeat
and do everything,

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.590
manually, you'll get very
tired pretty quickly.

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:39.480
And you'll start making
significant mistakes.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:43.320
So automation and
scripting are key.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:50.710
We already talked about breaking
down services to microservices.

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:54.810
[INAUDIBLE] services don't
lend themselves to scale.

00:19:54.810 --> 00:20:00.300
And when you're talking
about large scale, 100x,

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:03.450
you have to be
prepared, to redesign,

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:06.810
and rethink what you're
doing, and maybe rewrite

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:07.530
several times.

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:13.810
"The Debate Between Cloud
Managed and Self Managed

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:14.540
Kubernetes."

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:19.910
I'm not going to go
too much into that.

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:25.720
But all I want to
say is that using GKE

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:27.390
can save you a lot of sleep.

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:35.140
There is this one really useful
Kubernetes API called the Watch

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:41.710
API, which lets you monitor
the status of your pods

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:46.310
and get real-time events as
changes happen in your cluster.

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:49.720
So we're making use
of that in our demo.

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:53.275
Also we had made an assumption
that it is enough to,

00:20:53.275 --> 00:20:55.900
I just scale my
service in Kubernetes,

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:59.590
and then, traffic will
be distributed evenly.

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:03.420
And all the pods we'll
be happy to handle

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:05.140
an equal amount of traffic.

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:07.075
That is not the case.

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:12.780
You actually have
to do some work

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:16.240
to get proper load
balancing in Kubernetes.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:18.930
We were seeing that
some of our pods

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:22.140
were getting so much traffic,
they were literally, killed

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:23.190
and had to restart.

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:25.740
And others were
just sitting there.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:30.930
So Envoy proxy
solved that problem

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:35.640
using the Google Native
cloud load balancer

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.890
solved that problem.

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:40.120
I want to caution you.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.250
Watch out for the
less is more paradox.

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:46.060
So in our desire to
scale everything so high,

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:51.170
we said, all right, let's
run 100 of this service

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:54.340
and 1,000 of that service
and 2,000 of that one.

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:57.100
We even scaled one up to 8,000.

00:21:57.100 --> 00:22:00.820
And things actually
slowed down instead

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:03.350
of giving us better results.

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:06.280
So you really have
to think and optimize

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:08.680
for what is good
for your system,

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:14.090
and realize that,
sometimes, less is more.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:16.490
Last lesson I
learned that will--

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:20.960
actually, bring me back
to the next slide--

00:22:23.930 --> 00:22:29.420
is, sometimes one
Kubernetes cluster is not

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:33.360
enough to accomplish
what you're trying to do.

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:38.370
So when that's the
case, just use many.

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:42.740
So for the purposes
of today's demo,

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:46.715
we have built for you a
cluster of Kubernetes clusters.

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:55.620
What you see here is our
master Kubernetes cluster

00:22:55.620 --> 00:23:01.230
that has all of the
services that I showed you

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:06.940
in the previous
slide, scaled up.

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:09.910
And this, in itself, gives
you a scalable system.

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:13.600
Because you can scale
horizontally by running more

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.620
nodes in your cluster.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:19.060
You can scale vertically
by running larger nodes

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:20.540
in your cluster.

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:25.000
But what we've done gives you
another degree of freedom.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.500
Because you can run
as many Kubernetes

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:30.550
clusters as you want
to handle your load.

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:35.830
So now you can spread
your system really wide.

00:23:35.830 --> 00:23:38.350
And by the way,
remember how I talked

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:44.040
about self managed versus GKE?

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:48.190
Here we have done both.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:51.190
So it is not required.

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:55.950
We can run all of our
workloads on GKE or all of them

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:57.690
on Kubernetes.

00:23:57.690 --> 00:24:00.750
But we're going to show you
both just because we can?

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.820
So this is an example of
one of our GKE clusters?

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:09.970
It has 100 nodes.

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:15.270
And it's ready for us
to run workloads on.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:17.000
I'm going to zoom
out a little bit.

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:20.830
These are five GKE clusters.

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:25.720
So remember how I said
I'll make some analogies?

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:28.470
What does this remind you of?

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:31.990
Let me zoom out one more time.

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:35.350
On the top, live
five GKE clusters.

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:38.950
In the middle is our
management cluster.

00:24:38.950 --> 00:24:41.635
And on the bottom, are
five self managed clusters.

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:47.140
I don't know about you.

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:50.220
But to me, this looks
like a skyscraper.

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:56.270
OK.

00:24:56.270 --> 00:25:05.790
Let's take a look at how
everything looks on screen.

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.270
So I have split my
screen, here, in two

00:25:08.270 --> 00:25:13.580
just so I can show you
our management cluster.

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:22.630
I will-- wow, this
is really slow.

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:30.200
I just want to show you
that things are actually

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:32.860
running here.

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:39.000
I'm also going to show
you at what scale we're

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:41.640
running our core services.

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.460
And let me zoom this
out a little bit.

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:53.290
So you can say some instances
have 1,000, some 500 instances.

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:57.480
Now we have modified the
Kubernetes dashboard.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:03.720
So we can also visually show
you what the scale looks like.

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:06.750
And so we've added
this graph tab

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.370
to the Kubernetes dashboard.

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:11.370
And that is the
visual representation

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:12.430
of what we just saw.

00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:14.685
That's only the master cluster.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:21.850
I will zoom into this area here.

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:25.560
So each of the triangles
in this chart is a service.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:30.210
And each of the dots are
pods from that service that

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:31.500
are running in the cluster.

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:39.790
Let's take a look at
the worker clusters.

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:42.150
So here I have--

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:48.040
let me show you this.

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:58.950
We'll use a very nifty tool
called Kubectx lets you easily

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:02.490
show what are the Kubernetes
contexts that you're

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:03.700
looking at.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:08.790
So on the left, you'll see
each of these sessions pointing

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:11.890
to one of the GKE
clusters, 1 through 5.

00:27:11.890 --> 00:27:19.650
And on the right, we have 5 self
managed Kubernetes contexts.

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:29.390
So I'm going to show you what's
running in these clusters

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:32.345
by watching the command
Kubectl get pods.

00:27:36.020 --> 00:27:38.750
And what's running is
